., inborn error of metabolism): labs show normal electrolyte panel and glucose despite systemic symptoms", "Autoimmune disorder: lacks specific organ-specific signs or autoantibody context", "Vascular/occlusive etiology: no acute ischemic features in extremities or retina", "Endocrine crisis (e.g., adrenal insufficiency): serum electrolytes and glucose are normal"], "alarm_features": ["Constitutional B symptoms (fever, night sweats, weight loss)", "Hematopoietic triad (anemia, leukocytosis with blasts, thrombocytopenia)"]}, "branches": {"B1": {"id": "B1", "label": "Reactive / Non-malignant Leukocytosis", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "live", "prior": 0.18454440599769326, "posterior": 0.4570242551790839, "danger": 0.2, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": ["Subacute symptoms and normal electrolytes provide weak support for reactive leukocytosis but do not strongly exclude malignancy."], "evidence_against": ["35% blasts is a pathognomonic finding for malignant hematological processes, decisively excluding reactive leukocytosis."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.3, "interrupt_relevance": 0.1, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "mechanism", "representative_diseases": []}, "B2": {"id": "B2", "label": "Myeloid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.0, "posterior": 0.2355905640454636, "danger": 0.8, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["35% blasts is a pathognomonic finding for malignant hematological processes, decisively excluding reactive leukocytosis."], "evidence_against": ["Absence of splenomegaly or cutaneous findings weakly argues against CML in blast crisis but does not exclude other myeloid neoplasms."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B2.1", "B2.2", "B2.3"], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.7, "interrupt_relevance": 0.9, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "mechanism", "representative_diseases": ["acute myeloid leukemia", "myelodysplastic syndrome with excess blasts", "chronic myeloid leukemia in blast crisis"]}, "B3": {"id": "B3", "label": "Lymphoid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "live", "prior": 0.09688581314878894, "posterior": 0.22486763650787034, "danger": 0.75, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": ["35% blasts is a pathognomonic finding for malignant hematological processes, decisively excluding reactive leukocytosis."], "evidence_against": ["The lack of typical ALL-related neurological features weakly argues against lymphoid neoplasms but does not eliminate blast crisis subtypes."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.6, "interrupt_relevance": 0.85, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "mechanism", "representative_diseases": ["acute lymphoblastic leukemia", "lymphoblastic lymphoma"]}, "B4": {"id": "B4", "label": "Plasma Cell Disorder", "level": 1, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.083, "danger": 0.6, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["The absence of these findings strongly argues against multiple myeloma as the plasma cell disorder."]}, "B2.1": {"id": "B2.1", "label": "De Novo Acute Myeloid Leukemia", "parent": "B2", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.10470691735353939, "posterior": 0.10470691735353939, "danger": 0.75, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.7, "interrupt_relevance": 0.9, "level_role": "disease_class", "classification_axis": "etiology", "representative_diseases": []}, "B2.2": {"id": "B2.2", "label": "Chronic Myelogenous Leukemia in Blast Crisis", "parent": "B2", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.07853018801515453, "posterior": 0.07853018801515453, "danger": 0.85, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.65, "interrupt_relevance": 0.8, "level_role": "disease_class", "classification_axis": "etiology", "representative_diseases": []}, "B2.3": {"id": "B2.3", "label": "Myelodysplastic Syndrome with Excess Blasts / Secondary AML", "parent": "B2", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.052353458676769694, "posterior": 0.052353458676769694, "danger": 0.7, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.6, "interrupt_relevance": 0.75, "level_role": "disease_class", "classification_axis": "etiology", "representative_diseases": []}}, "frontier": ["B1", "B2.1", "B2.2", "B2.3", "B3"], "other_mass": 0.0, "pending_results": [], "actions_taken": [{"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Do the constitutional B symptoms (fever, weight loss) and blast percentage align better with a lymphoid than a myeloid neoplasm?", "summary": "B symptoms with 35% blasts show stronger alignment with lymphoid neoplasms (like ALL) than myeloid, though neurologic findings reduce this support."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Do the constitutional symptoms and hematopoietic triad (anemia, blasts, thrombocytopenia) more strongly suggest plasma cell disorder over myeloid neoplasm?", "summary": "The hematopoietic triad is more characteristic of myeloid neoplasms. Plasma cell disorders typically present with hypercalcemia or monoclonal protein, which are absent here."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the presence of 35% blasts in the leukocyte count argue against reactive/non-malignant leukocytosis in favor of a malignant hematological process?", "summary": "35% blasts is a pathognomonic finding for malignant hematological processes, decisively excluding reactive leukocytosis."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the absence of splenomegaly or leukemic cutis in the physical exam argue against CML in blast crisis as the underlying myeloid neoplasm?", "summary": "Absence of splenomegaly or cutaneous findings weakly argues against CML in blast crisis but does not exclude other myeloid neoplasms."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the presence of 35% blasts with normal visual acuity and non-focal neurology argue against ALL as the most likely lymphoid neoplasm?", "summary": "The lack of typical ALL-related neurological features weakly argues against lymphoid neoplasms but does not eliminate blast crisis subtypes."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the absence of hypercalcemia or monoclonal protein detection in labs argue against multiple myeloma as the plasma cell disorder?", "summary": "The absence of these findings strongly argues against multiple myeloma as the plasma cell disorder."}], "differential_history": [{"top": [["B1", 0.457], ["B2", 0.236], ["B3", 0.225]]}], "deliberation": {}, "interrupt": {"active": false, "reason": "", "required_actions": []}, "termination": {"ready_to_stop": false, "termination_type": "continue", "reason": "Leading branch (B1) posterior (0.457) <0.75, dangerous alternatives (B2/B3) remain unresolved with combined posterior ~0.46. No actionable parent-level management convergence exists due to distinct therapeutic implications of B2 and B3 subtypes. Further discriminators (e.g., peripheral smear morphology, flow cytometry, genetic testing) are required to resolve myeloid vs lymphoid blast lineage."}, "turn_budget_used": 1, "estimated_remaining_value": 0.8, "max_turn_budget": 5, "latest_action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "diagnosis_readiness_score": 0.4570242551790839, "benchmark_output_ready": false, "static_question": "Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "static_options": [{"id": "A", "description": "Acute lymphoblastic leukemia"}, {"id": "B", "description": "Acute myelogenous leukemia"}, {"id": "C", "description": "Chronic lymphocytic leukemia"}, {"id": "D", "description": "Chronic myelogenous leukemia"}, {"id": "E", "description": "Multiple myeloma"}], "seen_evidence_ids": [], "seen_evidence_phenotypes": "{'Myeloproliferative disorder', 'Multiple myeloma'}", "mode_policy": {"benchmark_purity": true, "allow_external_knowledge": true}, "answer_option_mapping": {}, "tool_use_log": [], "root_revision_needed": false, "program_faults": [], "max_tree_depth": 3, "reasoning_ledger": {"anchor": {"hypothesis": "B1", "t": 0, "posterior_at_anchor": 0.457}, "leader": {"branch_id": "B1", "label": "Reactive / Non-malignant Leukocytosis", "posterior": 0.457, "leading_since_t": 0, "n_revisions": 0}, "leader_evidence": {"confirming": 1, "disconfirming": 4, "last_disconfirming_digest": "35% blasts is a pathognomonic finding for malignant hematological processes, decisively excluding reactive leukocytosis."}, "action_intents": [{"t": 1, "intent": "refute"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "refute"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "refute"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}], "considered_alternatives": ["B3", "B2.1", "B4"]}, "discriminator_hints": "[Knowledge Layer: coverage=80%, source=both]\n\nLymphoid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts vs De Novo Acute Myeloid Leukemia:\n  Favours first:  abnormality of skin physiology, abnormality of the endocrine system, acrocyanosis, areflexia, arterial thrombosis\n  Favours second: acute myeloid leukemia, autosomal dominant inheritance, somatic mutation\n\nLymphoid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts vs Chronic Myelogenous Leukemia in Blast Crisis:\n  Favours first:  abnormality of skin physiology, abnormality of the endocrine system, acrocyanosis, areflexia, arterial thrombosis\n  Favours second: abdominal pain, abnormal/excessive bleeding, anemia, bone pain, confusion\n\nLymphoid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts vs Myelodysplastic Syndrome with Excess Blasts / Secondary AML:\n  Favours first:  abnormality of skin physiology, abnormality of the endocrine system, acrocyanosis, areflexia, arterial thrombosis\n  Favours second: abnormal bleeding, abnormal circulating albumin concentration, abnormal circulating protein concentration, abnormal mean corpuscular volume, abnormality of bone marrow cell morphology\n\nDe Novo Acute Myeloid Leukemia vs Chronic Myelogenous Leukemia in Blast Crisis:\n  Favours first:  acute myeloid leukemia, autosomal dominant inheritance, somatic mutation\n  Favours second: abdominal pain, abnormal/excessive bleeding, anemia, bone pain, confusion\n\nDe Novo Acute Myeloid Leukemia vs Myelodysplastic Syndrome with Excess Blasts / Secondary AML:\n  Favours first:  autosomal dominant inheritance, somatic mutation\n  Favours second: abnormal bleeding, abnormal circulating albumin concentration, abnormal circulating protein concentration, abnormal mean corpuscular volume, abnormality of bone marrow cell morphology\n\nChronic Myelogenous Leukemia in Blast Crisis vs Myelodysplastic Syndrome with Excess Blasts / Secondary AML:\n  Favours first:  abdominal pain, abnormal/excessive bleeding, anemia, confusion, easy bruising\n  Favours second: abnormal bleeding, abnormal circulating albumin concentration, abnormal circulating protein concentration, abnormal mean corpuscular volume, abnormality of bone marrow cell morphology\n\nRelated to Lymphoid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts: hematopoietic and lymphoid cell neoplasm, lymphoid leukemia (disease), lymphoma\n\nRelated to De Novo Acute Myeloid Leukemia: acute disease, acute leukemia of ambiguous lineage, acute myeloid leukemia with mutated npm1\n\nRelated to Myelodysplastic Syndrome with Excess Blasts / Secondary AML: hematopoietic and lymphoid cell neoplasm, myelodysplastic syndrome, myelodysplastic syndrome with excess blasts-1\n\n[Indirect reasoning chains (PrimeKG 2-hop):]\n  and night sweats → fatigue → Lymphoid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts\n  and night sweats → pain → Lymphoid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts\n  and night sweats → fatigue → Myelodysplastic Syndrome with Excess Blasts / Secondary AML"}
